        Why Counting Words in a PDF Is Uniquely Difficult
      </h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
        Unlike standard Microsoft Word documents (<code class="text-xs font-mono bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">.docx</code>) or plain text files (<code class="text-xs font-mono bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">.txt</code>), the Portable Document Format (PDF) was not designed as a text editor format. Created by Adobe in 1993, the PDF specification is essentially a digital canvas designed for postscript print fidelity. 
      </p>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
        In a PDF, words are not necessarily stored as paragraphs or sentences. Instead, text is positioned using coordinate-based glyph operators (such as <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">Tj</code> and <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">TJ</code> commands) that instruct the rendering engine exactly where to paint characters on an X/Y grid. Letters can be stored in arbitrary order, split across complex font matrices, or combined into typographic ligatures (such as “fi”, “fl”, or “ffi”).
      </p>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
        Our browser-native counter solves this by parsing each content stream via Mozilla's high-fidelity <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">pdfjs-dist</code> engine, assembling text fragments into natural semantic reading order, and normalizing whitespace before executing character and word calculations.
      </p>
    </section>

