    <!-- Chapter 5: Scanned vs Digital PDF -->
    <section class="space-y-4">
      <h3 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
        Digital PDFs vs. Scanned Documents: Understanding OCR
      </h3>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
        A common point of confusion is why some PDFs return a word count of <strong>0</strong> even when words are clearly readable on screen. This occurs because PDFs exist in two fundamentally distinct forms:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
        <li>
          <strong>Native Digital PDFs:</strong> Generated from programs like Microsoft Word, Google Docs, or LaTeX. These contain an embedded text layer where character codes correspond directly to alphabet symbols. These can be analyzed instantly.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scanned / Image PDFs:</strong> Created when a physical sheet of paper is photographed or passed through an office flatbed scanner. The PDF is simply a container holding bitmap images (JPEG/PNG) of pages. There is no machine-readable text layer until the file undergoes Optical Character Recognition (OCR).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
        Our tool includes a built-in diagnostic sensor that automatically detects when a document contains mostly images without a text layer, immediately alerting you so you know to run OCR on the file first.
      </p>
